Decode the pressure of an MPP v2 pen from its tone and its digital bits (Mpp2PressureDecode)

iptsd interpolates the position of the pressure tone between the first and the last of six rows
and reports it as 0 to 1. The pen steps the tone through 256 levels by dividing a clock
(22054884 Hz / (945 + level)), which is not linear in frequency and does not begin at the first
row, and it sends the force a second time: six bits in the rows 7 to 15 of the pressure window,
the lower four bits of the level and two more below them. All bits zero means no force, also
while the tone is still there.

Measured on the Surface Pro 9 with the Slim Pen 2 against what Windows makes of the same windows:
the interpolated value is higher by a constant 203 of 4096 (median), about 1.8 times the value at
light force, and it reports force for one more frame at the end of 81 .. 89 % of the strokes.

With [DFT] Mpp2PressureDecode the level is computed from the frequency of the tone and corrected
by the four digital bits, and the two lowest bits are appended (10 bits). The bits are only used
when each of the three row pairs has one clearly stronger row (more than PressureBitMinMag and
twice the other one), otherwise the window has no force. Result: the same value as Windows in
82 % of the windows with force, lower by 4 .. 28 of 4096 in the rest (an on/off bit that is read
as off here), never force where Windows has none. The eraser keeps the interpolation. Off by
default.

